WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
But I tried harder at Vietnamese, phrase
book and listening and listening to

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.670
material for a few weeks and stuff,
but still in Vietnam, it was unusable.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.470
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.550
Today I want to talk about language,
immersion and travel and how

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.960
that affects language learning.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.280
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.600
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.060
Uh, and if you're listening on
a podcast, uh, whichever podcast

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.890
it is, please leave a review.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:29.939
I do appreciate it.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.815
Uh, also I decided today, you know, I
always have my door closed and people

00:00:34.815 --> 00:00:36.675
are interested in my book collection.

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:42.315
And sometimes in behind I also have my
CD collection, uh, which no longer is

00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:47.235
relevant because you can download so much
audio content from the internet, but there

00:00:47.235 --> 00:00:52.125
was a period when I would be, you know,
I'd go to Riga, Latvia to buy Russian CDs.

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:57.035
I would buy Swedish CDs in  Sweden
and  Chinese CDsinn Beijing and,

00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:59.295
and, and Czech  CDs in Prague.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:08.340
Uh, whenever I traveled, one of my goals
was to stock up on books or, or CDs that

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.240
weren't available at home so that I can
then go and listen and read and I'd have,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.580
you know, available reading material
that was also available in audio format.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:23.649
All of that has changed now because we can
get digital content, uh, to read, import

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:28.725
into LingQ, uh, look up words and phrases
and we can get the audio content, which

00:01:28.725 --> 00:01:30.525
we can download and we can merge with it.

00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:36.435
So there's so many more ways to get
that contact with the language, but

00:01:36.675 --> 00:01:41.565
getting back to my door being open, uh,
sometimes people comment on my books.

00:01:41.775 --> 00:01:44.175
I just wanted to show you that
there are bookshelves in the

00:01:44.175 --> 00:01:47.205
next sort of area, corridor area.

00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:50.715
There's a guest bedroom over
there that's got more books.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:57.660
Downstairs there are four more bookshelves
with books on various subjects.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.420
So books are a big part of my life.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:01.320
Always have been.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.660
I enjoy going back and discovering old
friends and books are also a part of,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.850
sort of immersing myself in the language.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.900
I also do read in English, but if I'm
reading in a foreign language, I've

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.010
always found that reading a book is just,
it's just getting that language in you.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:21.225
It's just immersing yourself
in the language and, and,

00:02:21.225 --> 00:02:22.545
uh, audio books do that.

00:02:22.605 --> 00:02:26.055
And books that we read do that
because actually to improve in the

00:02:26.055 --> 00:02:27.915
language, you just have to get it in.

00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:29.655
You expose yourself to it.

00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:34.815
Now, obviously a great way to immerse
yourself in the language is to go to

00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:39.075
the country where the language, or to
a country where the language is spoken.

00:02:40.005 --> 00:02:41.475
And how does that best work?

00:02:41.535 --> 00:02:47.610
Well, my experience has been that
to take full advantage of doing that

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.100
you have to have a certain level.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.220
I would almost say like a
B1 on the European scale.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.680
You have to have put in at least six,
three to six months with the language so

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.580
that you can take advantage of being in
the country where the language is spoken.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.680
So you can actually
immerse yourself in that.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:09.285
So let's take the example of a,
of a country where, uh, you know,

00:03:09.315 --> 00:03:14.475
I visited and had no language
preparation let's say or minimal.

00:03:14.625 --> 00:03:19.485
So that would be say in my recent
travels, Myanmar and Vietnam.

00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:23.595
So I tried, I bought a little
book on learning Burmese and

00:03:23.625 --> 00:03:24.735
I had essentially nothing.

00:03:25.725 --> 00:03:29.805
Uh, and, and I came away with the,
uh, gosh, I can't even remember

00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:33.345
it, uh, the word for thank you
and hello, but that was about it.

00:03:33.915 --> 00:03:37.785
But I tried harder and Vietnamese phrase,
book and listening and listening to

00:03:37.785 --> 00:03:42.625
material for a few weeks and stuff,
but still in Vietnam, it was unusable.

00:03:43.035 --> 00:03:47.255
Uh, I could use my phone to dictate
something in English and out comes the

00:03:47.255 --> 00:03:49.275
Vietnamese and show it to the person.

00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:50.835
But essentially it was not enough.

00:03:50.865 --> 00:03:56.115
And so being in Vietnam, listening
to Vietnamese on television or being

00:03:56.115 --> 00:04:00.405
surrounded by people speaking Vietnamese
essentially did nothing for me.

00:04:00.705 --> 00:04:05.445
I would have been better off staying
at home and listening to material

00:04:05.475 --> 00:04:09.375
for which I had some texts, maybe
beginner material, even phrasebooks

00:04:09.555 --> 00:04:13.125
hearing them over and over again
in sort of a concentrated fashion.

00:04:13.365 --> 00:04:15.855
Being in the country, it
didn't do anything for me.

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:16.980
Okay.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.280
Now let's step up a bit
and let's go for example...

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:25.020
no, and I could add to that, uh, Israel,
when I visited Israel, I had even put more

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.600
effort into my Hebrew and it was hopeless.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.560
I just wasn't at a level where
I could use it or take advantage

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.730
of the Hebrew around me.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.440
And in Israel you can speak...

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.980
if it, if it isn't Russian, first
of all, English, and if they don't

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.480
speak English, they'll speak Russian.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.760
And in some cases they speak French.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.290
So there was no need to use it.

00:04:43.984 --> 00:04:47.375
It only became a problem when I was
trying to get my car out of a parking

00:04:47.375 --> 00:04:51.364
garage and all the instructions
were only in Hebrew and I was lost.

00:04:51.455 --> 00:04:54.755
I had to ask someone, but not
in Hebrew, I have to ask them in

00:04:54.755 --> 00:04:56.104
English or Russian or something.

00:04:56.705 --> 00:05:02.974
So that was, uh, and then of course
from Israel, I went into Jordan where

00:05:03.215 --> 00:05:07.085
again, I didn't have Arabic, but if I
went there now I would be better off.

00:05:07.085 --> 00:05:10.984
I could perhaps take advantage, even
though I learned standard Arabic,

00:05:10.984 --> 00:05:14.835
I would be picking up on some
things from their I guess Jordan,

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.530
Jordanian or Leventine Arabic.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.330
So that's better, uh, before going
to Romania and before going to

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.670
Greece, I put quite a bit of time.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.740
Uh, Romanian of course, was a lot
easier than Greek because Romanian

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.540
is written with a Latin alphabet.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:36.600
Romanian is 70% identifiable,
very similar to other romance

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:42.690
languages in particular, Italian,
uh, has some 20% Slavic type words.

00:05:43.695 --> 00:05:45.255
Uh, so it's a lot easier.

00:05:45.255 --> 00:05:50.414
So two months of Romanian was,
you know, helped me a lot and I

00:05:50.414 --> 00:05:52.275
was able to organize in Romania.

00:05:52.304 --> 00:05:55.635
First of all, I met with, we do
business in Romania, so I met

00:05:55.635 --> 00:05:57.495
with people there who didn't...

00:05:57.495 --> 00:06:01.005
some would either humor me in
Romanian cause they thought it was

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:05.054
kinda strange that, uh, you know, a
Canadian was learning Romanian and

00:06:05.054 --> 00:06:06.465
they were very kind and humor me.

00:06:06.854 --> 00:06:07.304
Or...

00:06:08.055 --> 00:06:12.945
I rented a car and for five Euro a day
in Romania, when I went there three,

00:06:12.945 --> 00:06:15.435
four years ago, uh, you got a driver.

00:06:15.825 --> 00:06:19.485
So I, I didn't want to be driving
in Romanian and run into a horse

00:06:19.485 --> 00:06:23.655
drawn cart or something and had
a driver for five Euro a day.

00:06:23.685 --> 00:06:27.315
For five Euro a day, I had a
driver, a guide and a teacher.

00:06:27.495 --> 00:06:30.795
So we spoke Romanian for
five, six hours a day.

00:06:30.795 --> 00:06:32.205
As I drove around the countryside.

00:06:32.295 --> 00:06:33.105
That was wonderful.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:37.140
In Greece, in Crete,
which is where we were.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.890
I had enough Greek that I
could actually speak to people.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.130
I could speak to people
in restaurants, not a lot.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:48.570
I couldn't engage in profound
conversations, but I was able to kind

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.430
of take advantage of being there.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.600
But it wasn't, I wouldn't say that
my Greek improved tremendously,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.060
uh, just by being there because
I didn't have enough of it.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.510
And Greek is more difficult
because there are fewer, you know,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.660
recognizable cognates if you want.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.370
So it wasn't enough to really immerse
myself to really take advantage.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.120
Now let's go to some other languages
where I have combined learning

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.070
with then going to the country.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.070
So, uh, Czech, I spend a
lot of time with Czech.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.670
Czech was, you know, I
did Czech after Russian.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.430
Oh, I did go to Russia after my,
you know, exposure to Russian.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:29.445
I went to Russia and I had, uh, LingQ
members there that I met up with in St.

00:07:29.445 --> 00:07:32.294
Petersburg and in Moscow.

00:07:32.625 --> 00:07:36.705
And I communicated with, I stayed
in these converted communal flats.

00:07:36.705 --> 00:07:41.115
And so I communicated with, with,
uh, the lady, you know, the heavyset

00:07:41.115 --> 00:07:43.604
lady that ran the places, both in St.

00:07:43.604 --> 00:07:45.525
Petersburg and in Moscow.

00:07:46.245 --> 00:07:50.325
Uh, and so I was using it and I was
operating and, and I remember going to St.

00:07:50.325 --> 00:07:54.515
Petersburg train station to, to buy a
ticket, to go to where I had a LingQ

00:07:54.575 --> 00:07:56.015
member that I was going to meet up with.

00:07:56.675 --> 00:08:01.055
And, and again, buying a ticket,
I know how to ask to buy a ticket.

00:08:01.055 --> 00:08:04.325
I know how to, I knew how to tell her
where I wanted to go, but of course she

00:08:04.325 --> 00:08:06.455
said something that I hadn't anticipated.

00:08:06.485 --> 00:08:10.565
So I was kind of thrown by that,
but eventually I was able to get

00:08:10.565 --> 00:08:13.475
across what it is that I wanted and
I got my ticket and that was good.

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:16.205
So those 12 days in
Russia helped my Russian.

00:08:17.025 --> 00:08:18.585
So after Russian was Czech.

00:08:18.795 --> 00:08:19.795
So I worked on my Czech.

00:08:19.815 --> 00:08:21.005
I did a lot of listening...

00:08:21.955 --> 00:08:24.235
and other great material on...

00:08:24.855 --> 00:08:26.535
And then I had a tutor online.

00:08:26.535 --> 00:08:29.955
In fact, I had two online tutors
and I deliberately set up with

00:08:29.955 --> 00:08:35.235
them that every day, first thing
in the morning, I jumped on a tram.

00:08:35.325 --> 00:08:38.729
I went to see the one tutor, Andre.

00:08:38.939 --> 00:08:42.959
And we were the, I spoke for an hour and
then I had another tutor in the afternoon.

00:08:43.229 --> 00:08:46.290
So that gave me sort of additional...

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.990
because you can't just
approach random people.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:49.530
I do that.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:50.099
I did that.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.560
I sat in the restaurant, there was
these two middle-aged ladies and I

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.490
kind of struck up a conversation, but
if there's a limit to how much of that

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:01.020
you can do, I also have some friends
that I could meet up with in Prague.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:07.035
So I kind of had between them, my two
tutors and being a little brazen about

00:09:07.095 --> 00:09:12.735
speaking to people, I did create some
speaking opportunities and of course I

00:09:12.735 --> 00:09:16.635
could read the newspaper and I could, uh,
if I'm listening or watching television, I

00:09:16.635 --> 00:09:22.065
would have enough Czech to take advantage
of that as an immersion environment.

00:09:22.785 --> 00:09:24.255
Uh, so that was Prague.

00:09:24.345 --> 00:09:29.925
I set that up, uh, when I went
to, uh, Leviev I had an online

00:09:29.925 --> 00:09:31.635
tutor and I set it up with her.

00:09:33.305 --> 00:09:36.305
That, uh, we would meet every morning.

00:09:36.334 --> 00:09:38.255
So this is actually a
phenomenal thing to do.

00:09:38.255 --> 00:09:42.454
If you can do it in any city that
you want to meet or visit, and...

00:09:42.524 --> 00:09:44.074
I was an excellent tutor.

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:47.525
And so she would meet me first
thing in the morning for coffee

00:09:47.675 --> 00:09:49.265
at nine o'clock at this cafe.

00:09:49.265 --> 00:09:56.055
And they, uh, the square there, uh,
And then we go to her, we'd go to a

00:09:56.055 --> 00:10:00.525
church or a cathedral, or if, you know,
there was a famous historical cemetery

00:10:00.525 --> 00:10:05.505
or whatever it is, we visited for five
days, three hours in the morning, three

00:10:05.505 --> 00:10:10.485
hours in the afternoon, she had her
iPhone or Android, whatever she had, and

00:10:10.485 --> 00:10:14.685
she would write down all of the words
and phrases that I had trouble with.

00:10:15.105 --> 00:10:17.535
I'd go back to my little room at night.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:22.410
And I'd get her email, which I would
import into LingQ and I would study

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.710
those words and phrases and we'd
go back out again in the afternoon.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.060
And I did that for five days.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:32.670
So I had a guide and a teacher, and
not only a teacher, but a person who

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.480
was giving me the words and phrases
that I struggled with so that I

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.050
could then learn them again on LingQ.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.940
And I think I can imagine, you know, I
tried to do that when I went to Kiev.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:45.000
I couldn't find anyone, but if you
can have a guide like that, who was

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.080
also a language teacher, I think
that's another great way to, you

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.940
know, make that immersion opportunity
in the country even more effective.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.950
And then the second time I went
to Ukraine, uh, I have people

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.920
that I was meeting both in Eastern
Ukraine and Western Ukraine.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:06.390
So I was in situations where I was
speaking Russian and Ukrainian a lot.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.800
So again, there, because there were
people in place that were gonna

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:15.390
help make that immersion experience,
you know, genuinely valuable.

00:11:15.705 --> 00:11:19.185
And, and I think I often hear people
say, well, you know, I went to Germany

00:11:19.185 --> 00:11:20.835
and people only spoke to me in English.

00:11:21.255 --> 00:11:24.795
And of course, this is going to
depend on which country you go to.

00:11:25.205 --> 00:11:27.255
In Romania that's less of a problem.

00:11:27.285 --> 00:11:30.945
Although the English level is not bad
there, but if you go to Germany or

00:11:30.945 --> 00:11:33.435
Holland, a lot of people speak English.

00:11:33.645 --> 00:11:37.395
So you have to have a fairly
strong level in the language.

00:11:37.455 --> 00:11:40.275
Otherwise people are not
necessarily going to humor you.

00:11:40.275 --> 00:11:42.005
People don't want to be
your language teacher.

00:11:42.345 --> 00:11:43.935
They have things to do.

00:11:43.935 --> 00:11:47.204
If you go into a store and they
sense that you don't communicate

00:11:47.204 --> 00:11:49.694
very well in Dutch or German,
they will speak to you in English.

00:11:49.785 --> 00:11:53.385
Most people, some people may humour
you, but you kind of expect it.

00:11:53.564 --> 00:11:57.495
So I've always taken the approach that
if I traveled to a country, just, just

00:11:57.495 --> 00:12:02.775
to summarize this sort of rant here,
uh, I should mention to be realistic

00:12:02.775 --> 00:12:04.185
about how much you're going to benefit.

00:12:04.185 --> 00:12:08.295
If you don't have a sufficient level
in the language, even if you have

00:12:08.295 --> 00:12:13.290
a sufficient level, it's worthwhile
trying to set up people you can talk to.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.329
Uh, and as, as I did in, in Lviv and
someone said, by the way, as a comment

00:12:18.329 --> 00:12:23.730
on one of my YouTube videos where I was
not so positive on language schools,

00:12:24.449 --> 00:12:27.120
and this person said, well, you were
very positive about your experience

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.719
at the Lviv language and cultural
centers, language school in Lviv.

00:12:32.069 --> 00:12:33.900
And of course I was because...

00:12:35.099 --> 00:12:36.510
accommodated my way of life.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.990
I don't think I would have
been as happy just sitting in a

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.790
classroom for six hours a day.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:47.400
So we got to make sure that we
structure that time in the say

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.060
theoretically, the immersion
environment in a way that works for you.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.320
So for some people it might
mean going to a school.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.444
Because you'll meet people and the
teachers will certainly speak to

00:12:56.444 --> 00:12:59.385
you in the target language and maybe
some of the other learners will.

00:12:59.535 --> 00:13:03.135
So that's also a matter of how you
structure it for yourself, but there

00:13:03.135 --> 00:13:05.025
has to be some planning put into it.

00:13:05.025 --> 00:13:08.745
You can't just go there and assume that
by being in the country, you're going

00:13:08.745 --> 00:13:13.245
to come away, uh, fluent in the language
that has certainly not been my experience.

00:13:13.275 --> 00:13:18.015
So I hope that's useful on this whole
subject of immersion and traveling to

00:13:18.015 --> 00:13:19.814
the country where the language is spoken.

